\par\par \uc2 Best Colleges of India 2026\par \b \uc2 Science  Pushing the envelope  Hindu College\b0\par\par\uc2 Shelly Anand \par \uc2 406 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 July 2026\par \uc2 India Today\par \uc2 INTY\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026. Living Media India Ltd \par \par \uc2 Specialised research labs, experiential learning help Hindu maintain a lead in innovative science education\par \par \uc2 Imparting lustre to Hindu College's growing reputation as a centre of research-driven, industry-oriented and interdisciplinary science education this year will be a set of novelties. For one, the National Mathematics Resource Centre, which will serve as a hub for advanced learning, innovation and mathematical outreach. Take, also, the AI Lab and a planned series of workshops, conferences and such initiatives to help students understand the growing role of \b \highlight22\uc2 artificial intelligence\b0 \highlight\uc2  across disciplines.\par \par \uc2 This year, the college is also expanding its specialised training facilities. The skill development centres (\uc2 SDC\uc2 ) in Mushroom Cultivation and Plant Tissue Culture and the flagship initiative, 'Sustainable Perfumery: The Art of Crafting Fragrances and Nurturing Careers', will be part of this. The SDCs will provide students with hands-on experience in Biotechnology, agriculture, food systems and entrepreneurship, while the certificate programme in perfumery is said to combine Chemistry, Botany, sustainability and product development, creating exciting career pathways in a rapidly growing industry. Following the success of its first batch of certified perfumers, many of whom have secured placements or launched ventures of their own, the programme now has strong industry participation and exposure to international expertise.\par \par \uc2 Science students will benefit from a growing ecosystem of specialised research facilities, including the Materials Characterisation Lab, Polymer Research Lab, Drosophila Research Facility, Centre for Environmental Studies, Green Chemistry Networking Centre and Design and Innovation Lab. Technology integration will be another big priority. The upcoming AI Training and Innovation Lab will provide students across disciplines opportunities to engage with AI, data analytics and emerging digital technologies.\par \par \uc2 Another key focus in 2026 will be increasing experiential learning opportunities. With externally funded research projects in areas such as Biotechnology, environmental sustainability, public policy, healthcare innovation, climate studies and advanced materials, students will be integrated into research teams at an early stage of their academic journey.\par \par \uc2 The college will also promote interdisciplinary research through projects in areas such as biosensors, environmental toxicology, sustainable infrastructure, healthcare innovation and climate-related studies. These projects will also expose students to scientific challenges where AI-assisted analysis, predictive modelling and digital technologies play an important role. The biggest change, feel students, is that now undergraduates, too, will be part of research and innovation programmes, becoming active contributors from day one of their higher education.\par \par \uc2 Document INTY000020260629em760000g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw117\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1755\pichgoal600
