\par\par \uc2 Best Colleges of India 2026\par \b \uc2 Dental  Crowning glory  Maulana Azad Institute of Dental Sciences\b0\par\par\uc2 Sonali Acharjee \par \uc2 612 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 July 2026\par \uc2 India Today\par \uc2 INTY\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026. Living Media India Ltd \par \par \uc2 With cutting-edge technologies, translational research and exceptional clinical exposure, Maulana Azad Institute of Dental Sciences remains firmly at the top\par \par \uc2 For students joining the Maulana Azad Institute of Dental Sciences (MAIDS) this year, dental education is increasingly extending beyond textbooks, laboratory work and routine clinical training. One of India's leading government dental institutions, MAIDS is a combination of a high-volume teaching hospital with a growing focus on research, innovation and technology-driven healthcare.\par \par \uc2 A major advantage for students is the sheer scale of clinical exposure. The institute treats roughly 2,400 to 2,500 patients every day, giving undergraduate students opportunities to observe and participate in a wide range of dental procedures and patient-care settings from the early stages of their training.\par \par \uc2 The coming academic year is expected to bring greater emphasis on emerging technologies that are reshaping dentistry worldwide. Students can expect exposure to areas such as \b \highlight22\uc2 artificial intelligence\b0 \highlight\uc2 , digital dentistry, chairside diagnostics, predictive oral-health technologies and translational research. Rather than viewing technology as separate from clinical practice, the institute is increasingly integrating it into everyday learning. "Students can expect a more research-intensive, innovation-driven and clinically enriched environment that prepares them to become competent dentists and future researchers, innovators, educators and leaders in oral healthcare," says Prof. (Dr) Arundeep Kaur Lamba, director-principal, MAIDS.\par \par \uc2 Research is becoming a larger part of the undergraduate experience. Students are being encouraged to participate in faculty-mentored projects, \uc2 ICMR\uc2  (\uc2 Indian Council of Medical Research\uc2 ) Short-Term Studentship (STS) programmes, clinical studies and interdisciplinary collaborations.\par \par \uc2 What makes MAIDS particularly exciting in 2026 is that students are no longer limited to learning how existing dental technologies work\u8212\'20\'14they are increasingly getting opportunities to help create the next generation of them. Whether developing affordable diagnostic tools, validating new technologies in clinical settings, or collaborating with engineers and medical researchers, students can work on projects that address real healthcare challenges. At a time when India is pushing for indigenous healthcare innovation, MAIDS is enabling young dentists to move from being consumers of technology to contributors to it.\par \par \uc2 One such example is the institute's growing innovation ecosystem. MAIDS is home to India's first Dental Technology Innovation Hub, established with support from the Department of Science and Technology and \uc2 ICMR\uc2 . The hub is focused on developing indigenous dental technologies and reducing dependence on imported products, giving students a chance to engage with real-world innovation and product development.\par \par \uc2 Beyond research and technology, the institute's long-running community dentistry programmes continue to connect students with public-health initiatives, outreach camps and collaborations with NGOs, offering firsthand exposure to oral healthcare challenges beyond urban clinical settings. International collaborations are also expected to expand in the coming year, building on existing exchange programmes and academic partnerships that have provided students with opportunities for global exposure.\par \par \uc2 "MAIDS as my alma mater gave me immense opportunities to learn, grow and succeed in academics and clinical skills," says Dr Parul Mutneja, MAIDS alumna and assistant professor and staff surgeon, Vardhman Mahavir Medical College and Safdarjung Hospital, Delhi. "Nine years at MAIDS helped me to discover the ultimate mantra of my profession\u8212\'20\'14ethical practice, empathy and patient welfare. My journey at the institute was rewarding and I feel forever grateful to carry forward its proud legacy."\par \par \uc2 For incoming students, MAIDS's appeal extends beyond its reputation for clinical training. It is increasingly positioning itself as a place where future dentists can engage with research, innovation, public health and technology while benefiting from the intensive patient exposure that remains central to dental education.\par \par \uc2 Document INTY000020260629em7600008\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw117\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1755\pichgoal600
