\par\par \uc2 Best Colleges of India 2026\par \b \uc2 Mass Communication  Master of the message  \uc2 Indian Institute of Mass Communication (IIMC)\uc2 , New Delhi\b0\par\par\uc2 Avishek G. Dastidar \par \uc2 405 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 July 2026\par \uc2 India Today\par \uc2 INTY\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026. Living Media India Ltd \par \par \uc2 More than six decades after its founding, \uc2 IIMC\uc2  is blending its rich legacy with AI, innovation and entrepreneurship to prepare the next generation of media and communication professionals\par \par \uc2 Established in 1965, the \uc2 Indian Institute of Mass Communication (IIMC)\uc2  has trained more than 9,200 students and produced generations of journalists, communication professionals and media leaders. As the media industry undergoes one of its most significant technological transformations, the institute is now positioning itself at the forefront of the next phase of change.\par \par \uc2 The clearest example is the launch of the first-of-its-kind AIME (AI in Media and Entertainment) Academy, an initiative designed to familiarise students with \b \highlight22\uc2 artificial intelligence\b0 \highlight\uc2 , synthetic media tools and algorithm-driven systems that are reshaping content creation, distribution and newsroom operations. The idea is to ensure that tomorrow's media professionals understand not only storytelling and reporting, but also the technologies reshaping both.\par \par \uc2 AIME is part of a broader expansion underway at the institute. Over the past year, \uc2 IIMC\uc2  has launched PhD programmes for full-time and part-time scholars and established an Incubation Centre to support student-led media ventures through mentorship and seed funding. Together, these initiatives signal a shift from preparing students solely for existing industry roles, to encouraging research, innovation and entrepreneurship.\par \par \uc2 The institute has also expanded its academic offerings. MA programmes in Media Business Studies and Strategic Communication, introduced two years ago, are attracting growing interest among students seeking advanced training in media and communication. At the same time, \uc2 IIMC\uc2 's legacy postgraduate diploma programmes, including English Journalism and Advertising and Public Relations, continue to draw students looking for a direct pathway into newsrooms, agencies and communication roles.\par \par \uc2 "Our programmes are designed to train media and communication professionals fit for the industry," says Vice Chancellor Pragya Paliwal Gaur. "The best part of the pedagogy here is an interesting mix of industry experts and academicians." Many faculty members bring professional newsroom and industry experience to the classroom, combining practical insights with strong academic grounding. "The \uc2 IIMC\uc2  curriculum is regularly updated to accommodate the changing trends of the media industry," she adds.\par \par \uc2 That philosophy is increasingly relevant in a sector being reshaped by technology. Through the AIME Academy and its Incubation Centre, \uc2 IIMC\uc2  is encouraging students not merely to adapt to disruption, but to help shape the future of media itself.\par \par \uc2 Document INTY000020260629em760000e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw117\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1755\pichgoal600
