\par\par \uc2 Best Colleges of India 2026\par \b \uc2 Social Work  The best in doing good  \uc2 Tata Institute of Social Sciences\uc2 , Mumbai\b0\par\par\uc2 Suhani Singh \par \uc2 423 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 July 2026\par \uc2 India Today\par \uc2 INTY\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026. Living Media India Ltd \par \par \uc2 In its 90th year, TISS drives its students from across India towards a richer understanding of society through fieldwork, conferences with experts and ideas-filled classrooms\par \par \uc2 Ria Avinash Mhatra, a third-year bachelor's student of social work, lives only minutes away from the \uc2 Tata Institute of Social Sciences\uc2  (TISS), Mumbai, but the campus is her "second home"\u8212\'20\'14one she prefers for its old-school vibe. Every Monday and Tuesday, she heads to a childcare institute to fulfil the course's fieldwork component. "Fieldwork exposed me to the realities of society," says Ria. "I was able to unlearn things and be more considerate of others' views."\par \par \uc2 Long known for its master's and PhD programmes, TISS has found new energy with BA and BSW (Bachelor of Social Work) students such as Ria. That energy is rubbing off on the faculty as well. "It allows us to think innovatively about our pedagogy and our engagement with students," says Bipin Jojo, dean of the TISS School of Social Work.\par \par \uc2 This shift has led to changes, including parent-teacher meetings and the use of podcasts and short-story writing as part of the teaching process. Faculty members are also encouraging the conscious use of \b \highlight22\uc2 artificial intelligence\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "There are benefits from AI in some areas, like forecasting projects, data visualisation, capacity-building of human resources, documentation and resource mobilisation," says Jojo. "What makes TISS unique is its blend of rigorous academic learning and immersive fieldwork, ensuring that theory is tested against ground realities," says Gopal Mitra, \uc2 UNICEF\uc2 's global lead on Disability and Development and a TISS alumnus. "TISS taught me to respect diverse perspectives and to question my own assumptions and biases. I continue to draw on these values and skills."\par \par \uc2 This year, TISS completes 90 years. In the pipeline are seminars, conferences with international experts, publications and a focus on areas such as youth, women and farmers. There is emphasis on multidisciplinary studies, ability- and skill-enhancement, and value-added courses in the undergraduate programme.\par \par \uc2 It is this variety that draws students such as Jagrit Yadav from New Delhi to TISS. "We write our reflections and learnings from fieldwork. It has made me more observant and helped me understand the whys." Yadav also appreciates that at TISS he gets to engage with master's and PhD scholars. This, combined with fieldwork and classroom learning, gives students a richer understanding of social realities and the skills needed to work with communities.\par \par \uc2 Document INTY000020260629em760000h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw117\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1755\pichgoal600
