\par\par \uc2 Best Colleges of India 2026\par \b \uc2 Medical  At the pinnacle of medicine  \uc2 All India Institute of Medical Sciences\uc2  (\uc2 AIIMS\uc2 ) New Delhi\b0\par\par\uc2 Sonali Acharjee \par \uc2 534 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 July 2026\par \uc2 India Today\par \uc2 INTY\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026. Living Media India Ltd \par \par \uc2 As medicine embraces AI and digital technologies, AIIMS New Delhi is blending innovation with clinical rigour to remain the benchmark for training future doctors\par \par \uc2 For generations of aspiring doctors, the \uc2 All India Institute of Medical Sciences\uc2  (\uc2 AIIMS\uc2 ) New Delhi has stood at the pinnacle of medical education in India. Yet its enduring strength lies not just in its reputation, but in its ability to adapt. As \b \highlight22\uc2 artificial intelligence\b0 \highlight\uc2 , data analytics and digital technologies transform healthcare, \uc2 AIIMS\uc2  is embracing new frontiers while staying true to the fundamentals that has come to define it: clinical rigour and patient-centred care.\par \par \uc2 Ask almost any \uc2 AIIMS\uc2  graduate what sets the institution apart, and the answer is often the same: the patients. With one of the highest patient loads in the country, students and residents are exposed to an extraordinary range of diseases, clinical conditions and treatment pathways. The sheer volume and complexity of cases provide a level of clinical experience that few institutions can match. The learning curve is steep and the workload demanding, but it is also one of the reasons \uc2 AIIMS\uc2  graduates are among the most sought-after medical professionals.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, the institute is adapting to a healthcare landscape increasingly influenced by technology. AI-powered diagnostics, predictive analytics and digital decision-support tools are becoming part of contemporary medical practice, and \uc2 AIIMS\uc2  is ensuring that students are equipped to work with these emerging technologies. But Dr Nikhil Tandon, the institute's director and a noted endocrinologist, says that the emphasis remains on using technology to strengthen clinical reasoning rather than replace it. "AI will undoubtedly become an important tool for doctors," he says, "but medicine, ultimately, remains a human profession. The best doctors will be those who know how to use AI intelligently without becoming dependent on it."\par \par \uc2 Research remains another defining pillar of the \uc2 AIIMS\uc2  experience. Earlier this year, it climbed in the QS Global Rankings, a reflection not only of its clinical excellence but also of the growing international recognition of its scientific output and research impact. Students are encouraged to participate in research from an early stage, across areas ranging from clinical studies and translational medicine to public health and biomedical innovation, helping bridge the gap between scientific discovery and patient care. Equally important is the institute's focus on community medicine. Students are exposed not only to the realities of tertiary healthcare but also to the social, economic and environmental factors that shape health outcomes. "India's healthcare challenges cannot be understood only from within hospital walls," says Dr Tandon. "Community medicine teaches future doctors to understand the realities in which patients live."\par \par \uc2 For prospective students, Dr Tandon has an important message. "Medicine should never be chosen simply because it is prestigious or secure," he says. "It is a demanding profession that requires commitment, resilience and genuine interest. If the passion is absent, the pressures can become overwhelming. But for those who truly want to serve, there are very few careers that offer greater meaning." It is a philosophy that continues to draw some of the brightest young minds in the country to \uc2 AIIMS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document INTY000020260629em760000f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw117\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1755\pichgoal600
